# What EAS uploads to build the phone app.
#
# EAS reads this *instead of* `.gitignore`, not in addition to it — which is
# the dangerous part and the reason the first half of this file exists. Without
# it repeating them, the ignores below would stop applying the moment this file
# was created, and an iOS signing key would be uploaded with the source.
#
# ## Why the server workspaces are not here
#
# EAS uploads from the monorepo root and runs `npm ci` there, so every
# workspace is installed whether or not a phone build has any use for it. The
# first iOS build died in that step: `services/deriver` depends on `sharp`, no
# prebuilt binary matched the macOS builder, and npm fell back to compiling
# libvips from source and failed. The Android builder is Linux and had a
# prebuilt, so the same tree built there minutes earlier — a difference that
# looks like flakiness and is not.
#
# Leaving them out is right beyond that one error. An iOS app build has no use
# for a Next.js site, an image deriver, a zip worker or an end-to-end suite;
# installing them is upload time, install time, and a supply of ways to fail
# that have nothing to do with the app being built.
#
# `npm ci` is content with this: workspace globs match what is present, and the
# lockfile entries for the absent ones are simply never reached. Verified
# against a clean `git archive` with these directories removed, along with a
# typecheck of the app itself, before this file existed.
#
# What must stay: `packages/`, which is where `@parea/autoselect`,
# `@parea/cards` and `@parea/upload` live, and which the app imports directly.

# --- the .gitignore rules, because this file replaces them -------------------
node_modules/
.next/
.env
.env.local
*.log
dist/
dist-test/
.expo/
.storage/
next-env.d.ts
*.tsbuildinfo
.DS_Store
.vercel
.wrangler
probe.mts
plant.mts
seed.mts
reseed.mts
cleanup.mts
design/unpacked/
/.env.local

# The iOS signing material. `credentialsSource: local` means the CLI reads
# these here and sends them to the builder over its own channel; they must not
# also travel inside the source archive.
apps/mobile/credentials.json
apps/mobile/credentials/

# --- generated natively, never uploaded --------------------------------------
#
# `ios/` and `android/` are prebuild output and untracked, so the git-based
# archive EAS uses *without* this file never saw them. A `.easignore` switches
# EAS to walking the filesystem instead, at which point they appear — 1.2GB of
# CocoaPods in `ios/Pods` alone, and a 437MB upload where 12MB had done.
#
# Size is the smaller half. A project arriving with `ios/` present is one EAS
# builds as-is rather than prebuilding, so the build would come from whatever
# `app.json` looked like when those directories were last generated locally —
# before the updates config, before `www.parea.photos` was claimed. A build
# that quietly ships an older configuration than the file it is supposed to be
# built from.
apps/mobile/ios/
apps/mobile/android/
.git/

# --- not needed to build a phone app ----------------------------------------
#
# Every one of these is anchored with a leading slash, and that is not style.
# A bare `assets/` matches a directory of that name at *any* depth, so it also
# excluded `apps/mobile/assets/` — and prebuild died on `./assets/icon.png`,
# the app's own icon. The mistake survived a local rehearsal because the
# rehearsal deleted the root directories by hand, which is not what these
# patterns do.
/apps/web/
/services/
/tests/
/tools/
/docs/
/design/
/assets/
